# vehje. Design (public API and composition)

The framework's public crate and front door. It defines the two plug-in
contracts a consumer builds against, re-exports the surface, orchestrates the
dev-time language compiler, and composes a distribution's targets at compile
time. No algorithmic logic lives here; it is where the crates the round split
apart are wired into the two-artifact compiler. `#![no_std]`, no alloc.

## The two contracts

- `Grammar`. The input-side plug-in: a consumer's front-end produces well-formed
  Core-plus-family IR. The framework mandates the IR contract and offers, but
  does not mandate, front-end scaffolding.
- `Target`. Re-exported from `vehje-codegen`: the output-side plug-in declaring
  `Supports` and `Permits` and emitting.

## Orchestration: the dev-time language compiler

The crate's orchestration is the dev-time language compiler, and it drives two
paths that share the compile stages:

- The per-program path. `run` takes a grammar's IR and drives it through
  `vehje-resolve`, the `vehje-check` graded judgment, `vehje-lower`, and the
  inclusion checks to a `Checked` residual, then hands it to
  `vehje-runtime-driver`. This is the path a bundled program takes.
- The per-language path. The language-compilation entry (compile_language,
  designed but not yet shipped, so named here in prose without backticks) drives
  a `vehje-signature` through the check and lower stages to `vehje-runtime-gen`,
  producing the validated-data package and its manifest, the language artifact
  the Zig runtime is specialised from. This is the path run once per language at
  build. It backticks once implemented.

The check-and-lower interleave (proof discharge folded into lowering) lives here,
in the orchestrator, routed through `vehje-ir` grade annotations: `vehje-check`
writes grade annotations onto the IR, `vehje-lower` reads the annotated IR, and
the interleaved graded fold is driven here, so `vehje-check` and `vehje-lower`
stay acyclic and neither imports the other. If the interleave ever needs per-node
alternation rather than phase-wise alternation, a `vehje-ir` fold-observer trait
is the seam.

## Compile-time distribution composition

State: partially built. The crate exports one reference target
(`DebugTarget`) and a `run` entry that drives the per-program path end to
end: it resolves the Core binders, runs the graded check, mints the
`Checked` witness through the target's family and effect inclusion sets, and
emits through the target into a caller-lent sink, returning a `Diagnostic`
that names the failing `Phase` on any stage error. The cheap lowering (ANF)
is not yet threaded into `run` (the check-then-lower sequencing is its own
gate), and the runtime execution hand-off is the separate downstream gate.
There is no distribution type, no multi-target composition, and no
compile-time linking of a target set in source. Target
composition across a set of targets is an owed implementation item. The design
below is what it takes when built; it is not shipped code.

- A distribution links the targets it supports; a generated match resolves a
  target name to a monomorphised emit. No `dyn`, no runtime plugin loading at the
  compiler layer. Target selection by name is a runtime string resolving through
  the generated match; the emit itself is monomorphised.
- `DebugTarget`. A reference target that emits a pre-order tag dump of the Core
  forms, exercising the whole pipeline end to end. It supports the `Core` family,
  permits no effects, and folds the checked residual through the shared
  `fold_core`, not as a production backend.

## The consumer-facing surface

`vehje` re-exports the types a consumer needs to build against the two contracts, so a grammar plus a family
plus a target is written against `vehje` alone rather than reaching into `vehje-ir`, `vehje-resolve`,
`vehje-typecheck`, and `vehje-codegen` directly. The re-exported surface, by role:

- IR building: `Arena`, `Builder`, `Node`, `NodeRef`, `NodeList`, `Literal`, `Span`, `FamilyId`.
- The family machinery: `Family`, `Core`, the `AccessSet` set constructors `Cons` and `Empty`, and the
  `Contains` and `ContainsAll` inclusion witnesses.
- The passes with their side-tables and family hooks: `resolve`, `resolve_into`, `resolve_with`,
  `resolve_into_with`, `Resolution`, `FamilyResolve`, `CoreFamilies`, `ResolveError`; `check`, `check_with`,
  `Graded`, `CheckError`, `FamilyCheck`; `GradeTable`, `Grade`, `EffectMask`.
- The output surface: `TargetSets`, `check_for`, `fold_core`, alongside `Target`, `Checked`, and
  `CodegenError`.

An end-to-end consumer demonstration (a minimal family plus target, built against `vehje` alone and run
through resolve, check, mint, and emit) is carried as an integration test: the experiment that proves the
framework hosts a consumer, and the reference a real consumer follows.

## Non-scope

- Any consumer's surface syntax, family, or targets. The framework ships the
  contracts and the composition, not a language.
- The Core passes, the output machinery, the wire ABI, and the runtime
  generation (their own crates).

## Dependencies

`vehje-ir`, `vehje-signature`, `vehje-resolve`, `vehje-check`, `vehje-lower`,
`vehje-codegen`, `vehje-runtime-gen`, `vehje-runtime-driver`; `hilavitkutin-api`
and `notko` for the primitives. `#![no_std]`, no alloc. A runnable reference
distribution, being the deployment host, supplies the arena-backing memory and
the I/O; the framework library crates stay strict.

